sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]))
from authoring import finish,ref,step,paragraph
finish('unconditional-information-inequality-decidability',
 id='TCS-6608',title='Decidability of unconditional Shannon information inequalities',area='Coding and information theory',criterion='decision',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Does a Turing algorithm exist that, given n≥1 and rational coefficients (a_S) for nonempty subsets S⊆{1,…,n}, always halts and decides whether Σ_{∅≠S⊆[n]}a_S H(X_S)≥0 holds for every joint distribution of n finite-valued discrete random variables?',
 definitions='The finite input lists n and all nonzero coefficients, each as a signed binary numerator and positive binary denominator, with its subset S encoded explicitly; omitted coefficients are zero. X_S is the tuple (X_i)_{i∈S}. Its Shannon entropy in bits is H(X_S)=−Σ_x p_S(x)log₂ p_S(x), with 0log₂0=0. Each variable has a finite alphabet, but alphabet sizes are unrestricted and are not supplied as bounds in the input. Joint probabilities may be arbitrary real numbers in [0,1] summing to one. No independence, conditional-independence, functional-dependence, fixed-marginal or support-size promise is imposed. The inequality is a single homogeneous linear inequality, with non-strict ≥0 and no affine constant, premise, disjunction or nonlinear entropy term. Runtime may be any computable function of the input length; polynomial time is not required. The number n varies with the input.',
 answer_criterion='An affirmative answer supplies a total effective procedure for exact validity of every such rational inequality. A negative answer proves that this language of valid inequalities is undecidable. Finding a false input inequality or proving an individual new valid inequality answers an instance, not the meta-question. A procedure that may run forever on valid inputs, or requires a promised numerical gap, does not resolve exact decidability.',
 context=[
 paragraph('For a given distribution, each subset of variables has an entropy. Collecting these numbers produces one point in a space with 2^n−1 coordinates. The question asks whether an input linear halfspace contains every such point, across all finite alphabets and distributions. It is therefore a question about all possible information structures, rather than about estimating entropies from a dataset.'),
 paragraph('Familiar examples are easy to express in this format. Subadditivity is H(X₁)+H(X₂)−H(X₁,X₂)≥0. Conditional mutual information is H(X₁,X₃)+H(X₂,X₃)−H(X₃)−H(X₁,X₂,X₃)≥0. Both hold without a premise. A negative coefficient is perfectly legitimate; it is the full linear combination, rather than each individual summand, whose sign is being decided.'),
 paragraph('The standard Shannon inequalities give a finite linear-programming proof method for each n. They express nonnegative conditional entropies and conditional mutual informations, or equivalently normalized monotone submodular set functions. If a candidate follows from them, it is universally valid. But the first non-Shannon inequality of Zhang–Yeung showed in 1998 that this proof method is incomplete. Rejecting an attempted Shannon derivation therefore cannot be used as a negative answer about entropy validity.','primary'),
 paragraph('The unrestricted alphabet quantifier is a major part of the difficulty. A computation on binary variables explores only a small portion of the permitted distributions. Increasing a guessed alphabet bound may discover new counterexamples, but an unsuccessful search at every bound examined so far provides no effective stopping criterion. A solution cannot quietly replace all finite alphabets by one fixed support limit depending on a heuristic.'),
 paragraph('There is a rigorous semidecision procedure for invalidity. If an inequality is false, it is strictly negative at some distribution with finite alphabets. Entropy is continuous on that finite probability simplex, so a sufficiently close rational distribution still violates it. Enumerate alphabet sizes and rational distributions, and compute certified entropy approximations of increasing precision. Eventually the negative value is certified. The arithmetical-hierarchy study of Abo Khamis–Kolaitis–Ngo–Suciu places validity in co-r.e.','decision'),
 paragraph('This explains precisely what a positive solution would add: a way to halt on every valid inequality as well. A complete effective enumeration of proofs of all valid inequalities, coupled with the counterexample search, would suffice. Existing automated methods can produce many correct certificates, but no completeness theorem for all rational unconditional inequalities is supplied by the inspected sources.','exploring'),
 paragraph('Taking the topological closure of the entropic region does not change this particular validity problem. A continuous linear form nonnegative on every entropic point is nonnegative on every limit point, and the reverse implication is immediate. Thus the same unconditional linear inequalities hold for entropic and almost-entropic vectors. This observation depends on the exact logical form; it does not authorize replacing the domain inside arbitrary conditional statements.'),
 paragraph('Cheuk Ting Li’s 2023 theorem establishes undecidability for conditional information inequalities and conditional-independence implication. Those inputs include premises that must hold exactly. Merely deleting a premise changes the statement. The theorem therefore does not by itself establish undecidability of a single unconditional inequality.','li'),
 paragraph('Adding a fixed penalty for violating a premise need not capture its limiting behavior. Kaced–Romashchenko explicitly show essentially conditional inequalities that do not extend to unconditional linear inequalities in the proposed manner. Their results explain why this seemingly direct route from conditional to unconditional validity needs additional justification.','conditional'),
 paragraph('Hannula’s 2024 work studies useful decidable simplifications: different classes of set functions and syntactic forms connected to database bounds. Its coNP-completeness result is for normal polymatroids, a restricted semantic domain, while another polynomial-time result uses monotone functions. These are precise complexity results for different domains, not a classification of validity over all entropy vectors.','primary'),
 paragraph('The connection to databases gives the question an algorithmic role beyond coding theory. Entropy inequalities constrain how large a relational join can be under information about its inputs and degrees. Related logical forms occur in query containment and dependency implication. Understanding which constraints admit complete reasoning procedures helps explain why some query bounds can be computed effectively while broader formulations encounter undecidability.','decision'),
 paragraph('The 2025 exploration by László Csirmaz compares the Copy Lemma, maximum-entropy constructions and the Ahlswede–Körner method, and leaves their completeness unresolved. Előd P. Csirmaz and László Csirmaz’s 2026 paper derives two infinite collections of five-variable inequalities and computes consequences through nine generations of its construction. Its enumeration concerns that method’s extremal inequalities; even the proposed characterization of the method is not a characterization of the entire entropy region.','five')],
 why='A basic computability question about the laws of Shannon information, with consequences for automated proofs, network and coding bounds, and database query reasoning.',
 importance=dict(score=95,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A foundational decision problem at the intersection of information theory, computability and database theory; it asks whether exact universal entropy reasoning can be fully automated.'),
 progress=[step('1998','Zhang–Yeung’s first non-Shannon inequality shows that the elementary Shannon proof system is incomplete, as recalled by the inherited source.','primary'),step('2013','Kaced–Romashchenko distinguish conditional and unconditional inequalities and their behavior on entropic versus almost-entropic points.','conditional'),step('2020','Abo Khamis–Kolaitis–Ngo–Suciu establish co-r.e. upper bounds for validity of unconditional and certain more general information constraints.','decision'),step('2023','Li proves undecidability of conditional information inequalities, network coding and conditional-independence implication; the premise-free question remains separate.','li'),step('2024','Hannula classifies several restricted semantic and syntactic versions and explicitly states the general information-inequality decision problem as open.','primary'),step('2025-09-28','Csirmaz’s revised exploration compares major inequality-generation methods and records open completeness questions.','exploring'),step('2026-03-03','E. P. Csirmaz–L. Csirmaz revise their five-variable work, giving two infinite inequality collections and an enumeration procedure within the proposed method.','five')],
 references=[ref('primary','Information Inequality Problem over Set Functions','Miika Hannula',2024,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICDT.2024.19','Inherited reference; ICDT 2024, article 19; §§1–2 define finite-valued entropy semantics and explicitly state general decidability as open; later sections treat restricted domains'),ref('decision','Decision Problems in Information Theory','Mahmoud Abo Khamis; Phokion G. Kolaitis; Hung Q. Ngo; Dan Suciu',2020,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2020.106','ICALP 2020, article 106; co-recursive enumerability and arithmetical-hierarchy distinctions for exact information constraints'),ref('conditional','Conditional Information Inequalities for Entropic and Almost Entropic Points','Tarik Kaced; Andrei Romashchenko',2013,'https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.5742v4','IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 59(11), 7149–7167; revision 16 August 2013; essentially conditional inequalities and closure distinctions'),ref('li','Undecidability of Network Coding, Conditional Information Inequalities, and Conditional Independence Implication','Cheuk Ting Li',2023,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11461v3','IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 69(6), June 2023; manuscript May 2022; conditional statements, not single unconditional homogeneous inequalities'),ref('exploring','Exploring the entropic region','László Csirmaz',2025,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12439v2','Posted 15 September, revised 28 September 2025; comparison of Copy Lemma and other construction methods, with open completeness questions'),ref('five','Information Inequalities for Five Random Variables','Előd P. Csirmaz; László Csirmaz',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23316v2','Computation 14(2), article 42; preprint December 2025, extended revision 3 March 2026; two infinite families and consequences through nine generations of a specified method')],
 status='source_open',status_note='Open for a single unconditional homogeneous rational linear inequality over all finite-valued distributions, based on primary sources and a later-result check through 11 September 2026. Invalidity is semidecidable. Conditional undecidability, restricted set-function domains and inequality-generation algorithms do not establish undecidability or decidability of this exact problem.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
